 The harmful mechanism is amplification, because engagement algorithms—automated systems selecting material likely to retain attention—increased time spent and exposure to uncivil and like-minded content, while a study of 126,000 Twitter stories found falsehoods 70% more likely to spread than truths and driven mainly by people rather than bots.
 Exposure is concentrated rather than universal, since research on 16,442 voters found that 0.1% of users shared over 80% of 2016 misinformation, but content moderation—human or automated rule enforcement—failed severely in Myanmar, where scarce Burmese-language capacity preceded a UN finding that Facebook was useful for spreading hate and responded ineffectively.
 Politics and information are consequently net detrimental: participation and cross-cutting-news benefits are reversible, whereas scalable deception, intimidation, surveillance, and hate can damage elections or vulnerable minorities, particularly in polarized, authoritarian, or linguistically underserved societies.
