+In economic life, platforms lower advertising, networking, crowdfunding, and customer-service barriers; Australian small firms adopted social media faster than larger firms, Mamanguape students accessed online courses, videos, and educational materials while businesses reported wider reach and employment opportunities, and influencer exposure helped the Memorial Day clothing brand become full-time.
 Those gains remain uneven by connectivity, income, and skills, while firms become dependent on opaque platform decisions: entrepreneurs described TikTok as useful for discovery and Instagram for sales but had to redesign and diversify content continually as recommendation algorithms changed.
 Public institutions also gain rapid crisis communication, as the CDC used Facebook during H1N1 to promote vaccination and correct the rumor that pork transmitted the disease, while expert-moderated COVID-19 forums answered questions and debunked misinformation for more than three million Reddit subscribers.
 Yet the same infrastructure magnifies dangerous claims: a two-wave survey of nearly 2,500 Americans associated increased social-media exposure with worsening vaccine misconceptions, and reporting on an early-pandemic study attributed at least 800 deaths and 5,800 hospitalizations to misinformation-driven practices such as drinking methanol or cleaning products.
